---
import CalloutCard from '../cards/CalloutCard.astro';
import CodeCard from '../cards/CodeCard.astro';
import PullQuoteCard from '../cards/PullQuoteCard.astro';
import PostFigure from './PostFigure.astro';
import type { POST } from '../../data/post';
import { ROUTES } from '../../data/navigation';

/** The unlocked article body: four sections of prose, figures and Koenig cards. */
interface Props {
	post: typeof POST;
	tags: string[];
}

const { post, tags } = Astro.props;

// Held in a variable so the braces survive Astro's template expressions.
const corrections = `{
  "corrected_at": "2026-03-14",
  "field": "subhead",
  "was": "four newsrooms",
  "now": "three newsrooms"
}`;

const bodyClass = 'text-body leading-body text-pretty';
const headingClass = 'font-display text-h2 tracking-heading leading-[1.2] font-medium';
const codeChipClass = 'rounded-[4px] border border-border bg-surface-2 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.88em]';
---

<div>
	<h2
		id='sec-1'
		class:list={[headingClass, 'mt-13']}>
		The audience stopped being a number
	</h2>
	<p class:list={['mt-5', bodyClass]}>
		Traffic was always a proxy. It was a bad one, but it was legible, and legibility is what sold advertising. When the
		proxy collapsed, the publications that survived were the ones that had quietly been measuring something else —
		replies, renewals, forwarded issues.
	</p>
	<p class:list={['mt-5', bodyClass]}>
		One editor described her dashboard as "three numbers and a folder of emails." The folder, she insisted, was the
		important half. Inline <code class={codeChipClass}>open rate</code> told her less than a single unsubscribe note.
	</p>

	<blockquote class='border-accent mt-10 border-l-[3px] py-1 pl-[26px]'>
		<p class='font-body text-[calc(var(--text-body)*1.06)] leading-[1.55] italic'>
			We stopped asking how many people read it and started asking whether the right forty would miss it.
		</p>
		<cite class='font-meta text-meta tracking-meta text-muted mt-3.5 block uppercase not-italic'>
			Editor, weekly culture publication
		</cite>
	</blockquote>

	<h2
		id='sec-2'
		class:list={[headingClass, 'mt-14']}>
		What a slower calendar buys you
	</h2>
	<p class:list={['mt-5', bodyClass]}>
		Cutting output in half is the most common intervention and the least discussed. Every publication that did it
		reported the same sequence: a nervous quarter, a flat quarter, then a step change in renewals.
	</p>

	<PostFigure
		src={post.inline1}
		alt='Minimalist desk with a lamp casting hard shadows'
		caption='A working desk at one of the four publications. Nothing on it was bought after 2019.'
		aspect='aspect-[3/2]'
	/>

	<h3 class='font-display text-h3 mt-11 leading-[1.28] font-medium'>Three edits, not one</h3>
	<p class:list={['mt-4', bodyClass]}>
		The house rule that appeared in every interview: no piece ships on its first edit. Structure, then argument, then
		line. Two of the four keep the passes on separate days.
	</p>
	<ul class='text-body leading-body mt-5 flex list-disc flex-col gap-2 pl-6'>
		<li>Structural pass — does the piece need to exist in this shape?</li>
		<li>Argument pass — is anything asserted that is not evidenced?</li>
		<li>Line pass — read aloud, always.</li>
	</ul>

	<CalloutCard>
		Disclosure: two of the four publications quoted here are past clients. Neither reviewed this piece before
		publication.
	</CalloutCard>

	<PostFigure
		src={post.inline2}
		alt='Minimalist concrete architectural space with tall walls'
		caption='The office none of them rent any more.'
		aspect='aspect-[21/9]'
		margin='mt-14'
	/>

	<h2
		id='sec-3'
		class:list={[headingClass, 'mt-14']}>
		The correction policy as a product
	</h2>
	<p class:list={['mt-5', bodyClass]}>
		Three of the four publish corrections at the top of the piece, dated, with the original wording intact. All three
		said it increased paid conversion. None of them could prove it, and all of them kept doing it.
	</p>

	<PullQuoteCard
		quote='A correction is the cheapest advertisement for your standards.'
		caption='Pull quote'
		margin='mt-12'
	/>

	<p class:list={['mt-9', bodyClass]}>
		The mechanics are unglamorous. Each of them stores corrections as structured data, which is what makes the display
		consistent:
	</p>

	<CodeCard
		language='json'
		code={corrections}
		margin='mt-6'
	/>

	<h2
		id='sec-4'
		class:list={[headingClass, 'mt-14']}>
		What I would steal
	</h2>
	<p class:list={['mt-5', bodyClass]}>
		The three-pass edit, the dated correction, and the folder of emails. None of it requires a platform decision, a
		redesign, or a new hire — which is precisely why it took four conversations to notice it.
	</p>
	<p class:list={['mt-5', bodyClass]}>
		The rest of the transcript, including the parts about pricing, runs in next week's issue.
	</p>

	<hr class='border-border mt-14 border-0 border-t' />

	<div class='mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2.5'>
		<span class='text-muted mr-1.5 font-mono text-[10.5px] tracking-[0.1em] uppercase'>Filed under</span>
		{
			tags.map((tag) => (
				<a
					href={ROUTES.tag}
					class='font-meta tracking-meta border-border bg-surface text-text rounded-pill border px-3.5 py-[7px] text-[12.5px] uppercase no-underline'>
					{tag}
				</a>
			))
		}
	</div>
</div>
